WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.510
One of the most influential periods in Black
American History post-slavery is the Harlem

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:10.000
Renaissance, an intellectual, social, and
artistic explosion centered in Harlem, Manhattan,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.610
New York City.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.679
Novels like Passing by Nella Larsen, Their
Eyes Were Watching God by Zora Neale Hurston

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:20.050
and the poetry of Langston Hughes were written
during this period and&nbsp; have become important

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.540
pieces of the American literary canon.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:27.340
Still, when discussing this topic we tend
to flatten the dynamic personalities and identities

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.480
of the Black folk responsible for making this
period so iconic in the literary sense.&nbsp;

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.829
Not only in America, but as part of the entire
Black diaspora.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:43.410
According to statistics before 1910 “more
than 90% of the African-American population

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.329
lived in the American South.” Due to poor
economic conditions and prevalent racial segregation

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.230
and discrimination, around six million African
Americans moved to the North, Midwest and

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:59.429
West, to places like Chicago, Philadelphia,
and Harlem between 1916 and 1970, a period

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:01.230
known as the Great Migration.&nbsp;

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:07.270
The first Great Migration (1916–40) is what
we are concerned with here today.&nbsp;

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:12.230
This massive geographical change was done
in order for Black Americans to gain a new

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:16.440
set of opportunities for themselves that were
not available in the South. Of course, the

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:26.610
North just had their own non-Jim Crow flavored
kind racial issues like Sundown Towns.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.900
In 1925 “The New Negro” anthology edited
by Alain Locke, the"Dean" of the Harlem Renaissance,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.950
was published. It launched a new literary
movement that spoke to the emerging new urban

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.360
Black identities that formed in the aftermath
of the Great Migration.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.300
As George Hutchinson, put it in the Introduction
to The Cambridge Companion to the Harlem Renaissance:

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.020
“The Harlem Renaissance in literature was
never a cohesive movement. It was, rather,

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:55.430
a product of overlapping social and intellectual
circles, parallel developments, intersecting

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:02.830
groups, and competing visions--yet all loosely
bound together by a desire for racial self-assertion

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:08.810
and self-definition on the face of white supremacy.
The interplay between intense conflict and

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:14.580
a sense of being part of a collective project
identified by race energized the movement

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.900
and helps account for our enduring fascination
with it”

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.680
Sadly, we do not have the time to go through
all the fantastic figures that made this movement

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.980
what it is, but we will do our best to ensure
everyone leaves this video understanding some

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:30.370
of the key voices of the Harlem Renaissance.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:37.769
The one you have most likely heard of is Langston
Hughes. Born February 1, 1901 (Aquarius) in

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:42.489
Joplin, Missouri, Hughes became a prolific
writer at an early age. He gained a lasting

00:02:42.489 --> 00:02:47.060
sense of racial pride and solidarity from
his maternal grandmother, who fed him a hearty

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.629
diet of Black Oral Traditions and stories
about her activist experiences.&nbsp;

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:56.609
He was especially concerned with the working
class Black people who were often neglected

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.870
in works trying to “elevate” the race.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:03.760
"The Negro Speaks of Rivers", which was published
in 1920, became Hughes's signature poem:

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.760
“The Negro Speaks of Rivers" (1920)
&nbsp;...&nbsp;

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.099
My soul has grown deep like the rivers.

00:03:07.099 --> 00:03:09.689
I bathed in the Euphrates when dawns were
young.

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:13.590
I built my hut near the Congo and it lulled
me to sleep.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.730
I looked upon the Nile and raised the pyramids
above it.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.609
I heard the singing of the Mississippi when
Abe Lincoln

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:22.180
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;went down to New Orleans,
and I've seen its muddy

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.109
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;bosom turn all golden in the
sunset. ...

00:03:25.109 --> 00:03:29.910
Hughes’s works would stress a racial consciousness
and cultural nationalism that sought to unite

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:35.159
people of African descent across the globe.
His writings would become an important centerpiece

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:40.730
of the Négritude movement in France and the
French-speaking writers of Africa.

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:46.260
Mark A. Sanders in "African American folk
roots and Harlem Renaissance poetry" says:

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:51.609
The sheer volume of [Hughs] writings, his
iconoclastic attitude toward black middle-class

00:03:51.609 --> 00:03:56.269
representation [...], and most importantly
his concern for the lives and culture of poor

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:00.909
and working-class blacks has made him the
veritable icon of the Harlem Renaissance.

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:01.909
(107)

00:04:01.909 --> 00:04:05.809
Hughes’s legacy is an important part of
not only Black American identity, but Black

00:04:05.809 --> 00:04:10.689
consciousness across the world. In his words
you see a love and appreciation for the beauty

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:16.150
of his people. That is what has made his poetry
so powerful for generations.

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.540
The night is beautiful,
So the faces of my people.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.680
The stars are beautiful,
So the eyes of my people

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:31.860
Beautiful, also, is the sun.
Beautiful, also, are the souls of my people.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.930
Born January 7, 1891 (Capricorn), Zora Neale
Hurston is one of the most important Black

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.840
female writers of all time. Period.&nbsp;

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.770
Initially from Alabama, Hurston attended New
York’s Barnard College and Columbia University

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:50.990
to study anthropology and do ethnographic
research, with a particular focus on African-American

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.240
and Caribbean folklore. (Me too girl)

00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:01.340
In 1936 and 1937, Hurston traveled to Jamaica
and Haiti for research, and drew from this

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:06.780
experience her anthropological work, Tell
My Horse (1938). Her work as a writer and

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:12.229
folklorist was used in Florida's historical
and cultural collection, expanding upon previously

00:05:12.229 --> 00:05:13.960
undocumented experiences.&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:14.960
Her most important and lasting piece of literary
work is without a doubt Their Eyes Were Watching

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:15.960
God:

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:16.960
“Honey, de white man is de ruler of everything
as fur as Ah been able tuh find out. Maybe

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.960
it's some place way off in de ocean where
de black man is in power, but we don't know

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:18.960
nothin' but what we see…De nigger woman
is de mule uh de world so fur as Ah can see."

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:19.960
Hurston's work slid into obscurity for decades,
for both cultural and political reasons one

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.111
of them was her use of African-American dialect,
which you can see in the previous quote. Some

00:05:21.111 --> 00:05:25.069
of Hurston's literary contemporaries criticized
her use of it, claiming that it was rooted

00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:27.599
in white racist traditions.&nbsp;

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:31.129
Richard Wright, in his review of Their Eyes
Were Watching God, said:

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:35.560
“Her novel is not addressed to the Negro,
but to a white audience whose chauvinistic

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:42.320
tastes she knows how to satisfy. She exploits
that phase of Negro life which is "quaint,"

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.870
the phase which evokes a piteous smile on
the lips of the "superior" race.”

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:53.860
Richard Wright and I …have beef.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:06:00.030
Modern academics like PBS colleague Henry
Louis Gates, Jr., have underscored the significance

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.590
of Hurston’s use of this language:&nbsp;

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:07.870
“The narrative voice Hurston created, and
her legacy to Afro-American fiction, [...] echoes

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:14.300
and aspires to the status of the impersonality,
anonymity, and authority of the black vernacular

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:19.010
tradition, [...] true somehow to the unwritten
text of a common blackness.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:25.740
Due to the efforts of Alice Walker and other
Black female academics, Hurston’s literary

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:32.370
star has risen over the decades and most recently,
Hurston's manuscript Barracoon was published

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.509
posthumously in 2018.

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:44.400
From Jamaica and considered the "foremost
left-wing black intellectual of his age" Festus

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:55.810
Claudius "Claude" McKay (pause—that is a
West Indian name), is a writer not as well

00:06:55.810 --> 00:07:03.710
known today, but was a huge influence on writers
like James Baldwin and … Richard Wright.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.759
Also, a Virgo.

00:07:06.759 --> 00:07:12.650
McKay came to the U.S in 1912 to attend the
Tuskegee Institute and in 1919 he wrote and

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:18.030
published one of his most famous pieces of
poetry “If We Must Die” during the Red

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:23.099
Summer, a year where white supremacist terrorism
and racial riots took place in more than three

00:07:23.099 --> 00:07:27.719
dozen cities across the United States.

00:07:27.719 --> 00:07:34.949
O kinsmen! we must meet the common foe!
Though far outnumbered let us show us brave

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:40.909
And for their thousand blows deal one deathblow!
What though before us lies the open grave?

00:07:40.909 --> 00:07:43.330
Like men we'll face the murderous, cowardly
pack,

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.990
Pressed to the wall, dying, but fighting back!

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:52.099
When the African-American museum was opened
in (year) this poem was read during the dedication

00:07:52.099 --> 00:07:53.099
ceremony.

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:58.090
McKay along with other Caribbean writers like
Cyril Briggs, Richard B. Moore, and Wilfred

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:04.189
Domingo, were influential as part of the Black
Socalist societies that were forming around

00:08:04.189 --> 00:08:05.189
this time.&nbsp;

00:08:05.189 --> 00:08:11.539
In 1928, McKay published his most famous novel,
Home to Harlem, which was both a bestseller

00:08:11.539 --> 00:08:17.889
and also controversial for being too sexy.&nbsp;
He attempted to capture the lives of “uprooted

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:24.729
black vagabonds” and did so with vigor to
the chagrin of many.

00:08:24.729 --> 00:08:31.590
McKay passed in 1948 and in 1977 was named
the national poet of his native land of Jamaica.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:37.560
Anne Spencer (another Aquarius) holds a unique
place in the history of the Harlem Renaissance

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:42.849
because even though she lived outside New
York City, she was still considered a key

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:45.670
intellectual player to the movement.&nbsp;

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:51.779
Spencer was a very well published anthologized
poet, and was the first Virginian and one

00:08:51.779 --> 00:08:56.490
of three African American women included in
the Norton Anthology of Modern Poetry. During

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:02.310
her lifetime, Spencer published over 30 poems,
but even still much of her work was lost to

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.980
the ages.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:07.899
According to scholar of afro-american studies
Carlyn E. Ferrari:

00:09:07.899 --> 00:09:13.860
In addition to providing a voice for black
environmental imaginaries, Spencer’s poetry

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:18.720
also challenges pervasive stereotypes specific
to black women and their bodies. [...] What

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.450
is unique and remarkable about Spencer’s
poetry is that she offers a vision of black

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:30.310
womanhood and the natural world that exists
beyond the ideologies of dominion; she offers

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:35.040
an alternative way of being and thinking about
being.” (186)

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:40.170
Spencer is unfortunately a lesser known figure,
yet a supremely important part of planting

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:45.060
the seeds of what would become the Black Womanist
movement. She is also a reminder that despite

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:51.589
the migration North, much of the Black history
we look to is rooted in the South.

00:09:51.589 --> 00:09:55.980
Her home became an important center of knowledge
for the likes of: George Washington Carver,

00:09:55.980 --> 00:10:00.750
Thurgood Marshall, Martin Luther King Jr.,
and W. E. B. Du Bois.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:01.829
Hot girl things.

00:10:01.829 --> 00:10:07.800
Nella Larsen (Aries) was born in Chicago to
a mixed race Afro-Caribbean father and a Danish

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:12.149
mother. Her father was absent in her life,
leaving her to be raised in a white immigrant

00:10:12.149 --> 00:10:18.490
family that taught her early on that she would
have no place in white society. This also

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:23.160
limited Larsen’s early access into a Black
identity. This disconnect would be the inspiration

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.850
for much of Larsen’s work.

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:28.850
According to Howard University professor of
African-american literature, Gregory J. Hampton:&nbsp;

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:31.060
&nbsp;
“It was not until the Harlem Renaissance

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:35.740
and the arrival of writers such as Nella Larsen
that realistic images of black women began

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.690
to evolve in the African American literary
tradition.”

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:45.149
Among her works, Larsen published two important
novels of note, Quicksand and Passing. Quicksand

00:10:45.149 --> 00:10:50.550
is her more autobiographical work, dealing
with Helga Crane who struggles to find her

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:56.870
identity in a world of racialized crisis in
the 1920s. She, like the author, has a white

00:10:56.870 --> 00:11:00.779
mother and an absent Black father.&nbsp;

00:11:00.779 --> 00:11:05.721
Passing, the more popular of the two, centers
the reunion of two childhood friends, one

00:11:05.721 --> 00:11:11.030
of whom is “passing” for a white woman
married to a racist white husband in Harlem,

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:12.030
NY.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:13.790
It … does not end well for her.&nbsp;

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:18.240
Also now a major motion picture.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.300
Larsen did not fit seamlessly into the New
Negro movement because so much of her work

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.790
dealt with the unspoken conflicts of not fitting
into your racial identity.&nbsp;

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:34.050
Larsen’s Passing is about the losses gained
from assimilating into whiteness and how for

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:38.490
Black folks it comes with more traumas. Not
only does it mean a loss of community and

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:42.699
family, but also a total severance of Black
history for future generations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.699 --> 00:11:46.910
Unlike the Rachel Dolezals of the world, these
people didn’t become white in order to take

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:52.639
over white institutions. They became white
to be given a fair shot to survive in a racist

00:11:52.639 --> 00:11:58.170
society. To simply be allowed to do what white
people were allowed to do. To be full citizens.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.209
It is a dark conflict that Larsen not only
understood as a person, but embraced as a

00:12:02.209 --> 00:12:03.209
writer.

00:12:03.209 --> 00:12:09.240
Before we wrap up, we also have someone we
should recognize in this story of the Harlem

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:15.760
Reinessance: Arturo Alfonso Schomburg, historian
and activist. Schomburg was an Afro-Latino

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:21.850
man of Black Puerto Rican and German descent,
proudly calling himself, Afro-Puerto Rican.”

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.820
After hearing from his teacher that Black
people had no history, he spent the rest of

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.440
his life trying to prove that teacher wrong.&nbsp;

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:33.620
He collected and documented the accomplishments
of people of African heritage all across the

00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:38.420
world. He immigrated to New York City in 1891,
eventually settling in Harlem.

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:42.800
By the 1920s Schomburg (Shom-burg) had amassed
a collection which consisted of artworks,

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:48.380
manuscripts, rare books, slave narratives
and other artifacts of Black history. He along

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:53.290
with Historian and&nbsp; civil rights activist,
John Edward Bruce formed the Negro Society

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:59.329
for Historical Research, which brought together
African, West Indian, and Afro-American scholars.&nbsp;

00:12:59.329 --> 00:13:03.290
Schomburg’s collection is part of the Schomburg
Center for Research in Black Culture which

00:13:03.290 --> 00:13:04.589
is located in Harlem today.

00:13:04.589 --> 00:13:09.630
One of the frustrating parts of Black History
Month is that it stuffs so much important

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:15.790
history and legacy into the shortest month
of the year that you don’t have the time

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:20.150
to get into the meat of things. I never knew
how many queer Black people were part of the

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:24.370
Harlem Renissance, the infighting between
important figures, the structures of Black

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:29.000
feminism that were being built and the way
it melded Black American and Black Immigrant

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.030
stuggles in a major city.

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:35.560
I’m from Brooklyn. I’ve been to Harlem
so many times and yet, it wasn’t until I

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:40.120
started doing my own research that I got a
real understanding of this history.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:44.653
This video isn’t just a quick overview of
the greatest hits of a literary era. It is

00:13:44.653 --> 00:13:50.910
an invitation to do your own research and
read up on these truly interesting players

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:55.720
in one of the golden ages of Black Intelligentsia.
You will not regret it, there is a lot of

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:04.040
drama. And also a lot of important history
that matters 365 days a year … not just

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:08.619
in February.&nbsp;

